[{:db/id "activity-8d3adf3f-e138-58ac-be7c-f8a39b171e26" :activity/id #uuid "8d3adf3f-e138-58ac-be7c-f8a39b171e26" :activity/title "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050707 :activity/course 17592186046673 :activity/steps ["step-ab6e41fa-e577-543c-8c44-dd8e57314fc7" "step-3faafc6b-71b5-511b-9711-f9114709af7a" "step-7fcb2994-7f35-5585-b8ef-6c814be8d434" "step-c8fbc123-cd40-50c1-9ac9-254d6e8dd576" "step-6996d827-5862-511f-81db-81953235cdc4"] :activity/first-step "step-ab6e41fa-e577-543c-8c44-dd8e57314fc7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ab6e41fa-e577-543c-8c44-dd8e57314fc7" :step/id #uuid "ab6e41fa-e577-543c-8c44-dd8e57314fc7" :step/content 17592186308688 :step/next "step-3faafc6b-71b5-511b-9711-f9114709af7a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3faafc6b-71b5-511b-9711-f9114709af7a" :step/id #uuid "3faafc6b-71b5-511b-9711-f9114709af7a" :step/content 17592186308696 :step/next "step-7fcb2994-7f35-5585-b8ef-6c814be8d434" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7fcb2994-7f35-5585-b8ef-6c814be8d434" :step/id #uuid "7fcb2994-7f35-5585-b8ef-6c814be8d434" :step/content 17592186479848 :step/next "step-c8fbc123-cd40-50c1-9ac9-254d6e8dd576" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c8fbc123-cd40-50c1-9ac9-254d6e8dd576" :step/id #uuid "c8fbc123-cd40-50c1-9ac9-254d6e8dd576" :step/content 17592186308724 :step/next "step-6996d827-5862-511f-81db-81953235cdc4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6996d827-5862-511f-81db-81953235cdc4" :step/id #uuid "6996d827-5862-511f-81db-81953235cdc4" :step/content 17592186396565 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4cb48e08-10b0-5815-97ab-c688f0460cd6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4cb48e08-10b0-5815-97ab-c688f0460cd6" :question/id #uuid "4cb48e08-10b0-5815-97ab-c688f0460cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132606" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{(1/2)} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & ={f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2})(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4y-2 \\\\ z & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4y-1$ (or $4y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,4⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f1aab5e-5cef-5890-b716-82e71a46cb8a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f1aab5e-5cef-5890-b716-82e71a46cb8a" :answer-field/id #u