he limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{(3-\\sqrt{3x})(3+\\sqrt{3x})}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{{3}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3x})}^{2}}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{9-3x}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{-3(x-3)}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{-3}{3+\\sqrt{3x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3+\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c38e3c1-1d1c-5a27-b17b-40b29b8fb328"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c38e3c1-1d1c-5a27-b17b-40b29b8fb328" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c38e3c1-1d1c-5a27-b17b-40b29b8fb328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7480c9f6-e405-5332-a8c3-73853a075b11" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7480c9f6-e405-5332-a8c3-73853a075b11" "answer-0b03e9b7-6868-53d3-a895-8e4fdbdf9bc1" "answer-22efaf2d-5390-5601-8a1b-287b90036eae" "answer-99493345-01cb-5276-9363-0b8dd99f66ee" "answer-a026f1d8-6c30-50f1-91a2-41e1f6bd150f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7480c9f6-e405-5332-a8c3-73853a075b11" :answer/id #uuid "7480c9f6-e405-5332-a8c3-73853a075b11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b03e9b7-6868-53d3-a895-8e4fdbdf9bc1" :answer/id #uuid "0b03e9b7-6868-53d3-a895-8e4fdbdf9bc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22efaf2d-5390-5601-8a1b-287b90036eae" :answer/id #uuid "22efaf2d-5390-5601-8a1b-287b90036eae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99493345-01cb-5276-9363-0b8dd99f66ee" :answer/id #uuid "99493345-01cb-5276-9363-0b8dd99f66ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a026f1d8-6c30-50f1-91a2-41e1f6bd150f" :answer/id #uuid "a026f1d8-6c30-50f1-91a2-41e1f6bd150f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-782d71a6-13dd-5aa9-bf77-a0e9f83d71b5" :question/id #uuid "782d71a6-13dd-5aa9-bf77-a0e9f83d71b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-338468" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{1}^{2}}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2d8564e2-8428-52a2-bc1e-e897b6b73c0b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2d8564e2-8428-52a2-bc1e-e897b6b73c0b" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8564e2-8428-52a2-bc1e-e897b6b73c0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e713aa29-49dc-5774-8f3d-14d36ee8df02" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e713aa29-49dc-5774-8f3d-14d36ee8df02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e713aa29-49dc-5774-8f3d-14d36ee8df02" :answer/id #uuid "e713aa29-49dc-5774-8f3d-14d36ee8df02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13cd01f5-b70e-51c8-add8-d0b755916801" :question/id #uuid "13cd01f5-b70e-51c8-add8-d0b755916801" :question/math-academy-id "q-46832" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & =\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{4}}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(2-\\sqrt{x})}{x-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(2-\\sqrt{x})(2+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4({2}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2})}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(4-x)}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{-4(x-4)}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{-4}{2+\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2+\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16a13345-7a00-5a93-b9a2-544eb1b7c369"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16a13345-7a00-5a93-b9a2-544eb1b7c369" :answer-field/id #uuid "16a13345-7a00-5a93-b9a2-544eb1b7c369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b179e100-1a56-51bc-9676-7dda7eb65e12" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b179e100-1a56-51bc-9676-7dda7eb65e12" "answer-ae65fcbc-3006-5c14-89e6-8ebed74524ec" "answer-cd4c2b31-de7b-5189-866e-8f0ddf3316c8" "answer-6d1c6da9-f3e0-545e-99b7-211c6ba45633" "answer-f8a8608e-e15b-5350-9981-cd57b649d33d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b179e100-1a56-51bc-9676-7dda7eb65e12" :answer/id #uuid "b179e100-1a56-51bc-9676-7dda7eb65e12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae65fcbc-3006-5c14-89e6-8ebed74524ec" :answer/id #uuid "ae65fcbc-3006-5c14-89e6-8ebed74524ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd4c2b31-de7b-5189-866e-8f0ddf3316c8" :answer/id #uuid "cd4c2b31-de7b-5189-866e-8f0ddf3316c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d1c6da9-f3e0-545e-99b7-211c6ba45633" :answer/id #uuid "6d1c6da9-f3e0-545e-99b7-211c6ba45633" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8a8608e-e15b-5350-9981-cd57b649d33d" :answer/id #uuid "f8a8608e-e15b-5350-9981-cd57b649d33d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078557 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-386a05f8-a2c4-54b1-8442-7cec1d9c62b6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-386a05f8-a2c4-54b1-8442-7cec1d9c62b6" :question/id #uuid "386a05f8-a2c4-54b1-8442-7cec1d9c62b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-338523" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}} & =\\frac{1-1}{\\sqrt{3(1)+2}-\\sqrt{4(1)+1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1})(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(\\sqrt{3x+2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{4x+1})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(3x+2)-(4x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-(1-x)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{1-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}[-(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})] \\\\  & =-(\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad2d4819-5891-5f49-ae40-2b183406271e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad2d4819-5891-5f49-ae40-2b183406271e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2d4819-5891-5f49-ae40-2b183406271e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7e07e547-aa89-5cae-8d95-54625e3c375f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7e07e547-aa89-5cae-8d95-54625e3c375f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e07e547-aa89-5cae-8d95-54625e3c375f" :answer/id #uuid "7e07e547-aa89-5cae-8d95-54625e3c375f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078953 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079646 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 1} \\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 1}{x - 1}\n$$\n\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting $x=1,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator of the function by the **conjugate** of the numerator.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of $\\sqrt{x}-1,$ we simply switch the sign between the two terms to get $\\sqrt{x}+ 1.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\require{cancel}\n\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 1} {x - 1}\n\t&= \\lim_{x\\to 1} \\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 1} {x - 1} \\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x} + 1}{\\sqrt{x} + 1}\t\\\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{(\\sqrt{x} - 1)(\\sqrt{x} +1)} {(x-1)(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\\\n        &=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2 - 1^2} {(x-1)(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{x-1} {(x-1)(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{\\cancel{x-1}} {\\cancel{(x-1)}(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac1{\\sqrt{x} + 1}\\\\\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1} +1}\\\\\n\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078635 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5fa4d8a0-3fad-569b-a952-bc2efe6c446d" "question-1735602f-bd75-58b9-8473-899a4cca6ec8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5fa4d8a0-3fad-569b-a952-bc2efe6c446d" :question/id #uuid "5fa4d8a0-3fad-569b-a952-bc2efe6c446d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152269" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=21-4x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}-{y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2} \\\\ y &=21-4x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =21-4x \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-21 & =0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-7,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-7$ and ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(-7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,49).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(3)^{2} \\\\  & =9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,9).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=49,\\,{y}_{2}=9,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}=49-9=40.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8ca9ba76d66d80a0a564fbefd02342d3f63d99f6fbd907b736cdc9acee79b174.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15617f41-35a9-548a-b217-4bc5608003fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15617f41-35a9-548a-b217-4bc5608003fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "15617f41-35a9-548a-b217-4bc5608003fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f4b47cc5-e313-59aa-949c-897284af5182" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c55f1198-aad6-521b-bae5-b64244ff77e5" "answer-16f6109b-7508-5135-8923-7bab55919240" "answer-cef3c9ba-eb28-5a61-91c0-f5a03b59dee0" "answer-f4b47cc5-e313-59aa-949c-897284af5182" "answer-12845067-a8b7-5913-979e-1299091f1d0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c55f1198-aad6-521b-bae5-b64244ff77e5" :answer/id #uuid "c55f1198-aad6-521b-bae5-b64244ff77e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16f6109b-7508-5135-8923-7bab55919240" :answer/id #uuid "16f6109b-7508-5135-8923-7bab55919240" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cef3c9ba-eb28-5a61-91c0-f5a03b59dee0" :answer/id #uuid "cef3c9ba-eb28-5a61-91c0-f5a03b59dee0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4b47cc5-e313-59aa-949c-897284af5182" :answer/id #uuid "f4b47cc5-e313-59aa-949c-897284af5182" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12845067-a8b7-5913-979e-1299091f1d0f" :answer/id #uuid "12845067-a8b7-5913-979e-1299091f1d0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1735602f-bd75-58b9-8473-899a4cca6ec8" :question/id #uuid "1735602f-bd75-58b9-8473-899a4cca6ec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-152280" :question/problem "The parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and the line $y=2x+1$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ y &=2x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2{x}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =2(0)+1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =2{x}_{2}+1 \\\\  & =2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=3,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=1+3=4.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7751473a1f9c40086a3a765487cf24f8e5430150d015cd64f1ed0c5bd62fcee4.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-978d262c-3412-5d34-a3c8-3416c0e3c873"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-978d262c-3412-5d34-a3c8-3416c0e3c873" :answer-field/id #uuid "978d262c-3412-5d34-a3c8-3416c0e3c873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d37f8a21-89a6-581f-bd81-65529db39d63" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d93872e-5c7a-54f9-bdf4-ce3a1167345a" "answer-40949b7c-a27c-5611-b404-6149a0b8d050" "answer-d37f8a21-89a6-581f-bd81-65529db39d63" "answer-adcb1ac8-48fc-566e-a31f-2be7b6ee6dc6" "answer-b4792ac3-9031-5945-8164-12455d78b667"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d93872e-5c7a-54f9-bdf4-ce3a1167345a" :answer/id #uuid "4d93872e-5c7a-54f9-bdf4-ce3a1167345a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40949b7c-a27c-5611-b404-6149a0b8d050" :answer/id #uuid "40949b7c-a27c-5611-b404-6149a0b8d050" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d37f8a21-89a6-581f-bd81-65529db39d63" :answer/id #uuid "d37f8a21-89a6-581f-bd81-65529db39d63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adcb1ac8-48fc-566e-a31f-2be7b6ee6dc6" :answer/id #uuid "adcb1ac8-48fc-566e-a31f-2be7b6ee6dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4792ac3-9031-5945-8164-12455d78b667" :answer/id #uuid "b4792ac3-9031-5945-8164-12455d78b667" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-569846cc-609e-5d75-9d24-71aff484c31e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-569846cc-609e-5d75-9d24-71aff484c31e" :question/id #uuid "569846cc-609e-5d75-9d24-71aff484c31e" :question/math-academy-id "q-155535" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+3x-1$ and the line $y=-3x-10$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2}+3x-1 \\\\ y &=-3x-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =-3x-10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x-1+3x+10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-3.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. \nLet's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-10 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-10 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the only point of intersection is $(-3,-1).$ Therefore, $p=-3,\\,q=-1,$ and \n\n$$\np+q=-3+(-1)=-4.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/91/91bedb41cd1b30cf392b23c0986e7b191ecf170fbdd4a0c34f5e2b8977c747b2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-91cdd488-37fe-52d7-879d-76d1056cf280"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-91cdd488-37fe-52d7-879d-76d1056cf280" :answer-field/id #uuid "91cdd488-37fe-52d7-879d-76d1056cf280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-691119d4-06b9-55bd-8f14-b3a8e2f8e06f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-19f0c16c-499b-5a14-9e19-173e4d918a84" "answer-1db6d43d-85f2-5fa4-ae72-336840f36edb" "answer-691119d4-06b9-55bd-8f14-b3a8e2f8e06f" "answer-5594fa57-8458-511c-9aeb-7fe140e03b18" "answer-abc497cf-9291-5541-a458-1cc9c303bee7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19f0c16c-499b-5a14-9e19-173e4d918a84" :answer/id #uuid "19f0c16c-499b-5a14-9e19-173e4d918a84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1db6d43d-85f2-5fa4-ae72-336840f36edb" :answer/id #uuid "1db6d43d-85f2-5fa4-ae72-336840f36edb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-691119d4-06b9-55bd-8f14-b3a8e2f8e06f" :answer/id #uuid "691119d4-06b9-55bd-8f14-b3a8e2f8e06f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5594fa57-8458-511c-9aeb-7fe140e03b18" :answer/id #uuid "5594fa57-8458-511c-9aeb-7fe140e03b18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abc497cf-9291-5541-a458-1cc9c303bee7" :answer/id #uuid "abc497cf-9291-5541-a458-1cc9c303bee7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-03601169-5838-5406-9812-fc2ccaab4423" "question-768c8258-584d-53be-9ed2-7212820baebf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-03601169-5838-5406-9812-fc2ccaab4423" :question/id #uuid "03601169-5838-5406-9812-fc2ccaab4423" :question/math-academy-id "q-155959" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x+2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x+2k$ intersects the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2{x}^{2}-x \\\\ y &=x+2k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-x & =x+2k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=2,\\,b=-2,$ and $c=-2k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(2)(-2k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4+16k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+16k & <0 \\\\ 16k & <-4 \\\\ k & <-\\frac{4}{16} \\\\ k & <-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7734cd7d-32e7-59ba-99af-993307dacc38"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7734cd7d-32e7-59ba-99af-993307dacc38" :answer-field/id #uuid "7734cd7d-32e7-59ba-99af-993307dacc38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fb3e5360-5da9-5043-989a-9ac296e89f54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-79ab7361-c55a-518b-a40b-db0377da228a" "answer-fb3e5360-5da9-5043-989a-9ac296e89f54" "answer-bcc8b0a8-1117-56ef-a3e6-79add5f0af91" "answer-31701e1b-752e-53b5-87f8-c950c55efe89" "answer-af5a6d7b-5c27-5373-b0c3-f3df6c003fb7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79ab7361-c55a-518b-a40b-db0377da228a" :answer/id #uuid "79ab7361-c55a-518b-a40b-db0377da228a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb3e5360-5da9-5043-989a-9ac296e89f54" :answer/id #uuid "fb3e5360-5da9-5043-989a-9ac296e89f54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcc8b0a8-1117-56ef-a3e6-79add5f0af91" :answer/id #uuid "bcc8b0a8-1117-56ef-a3e6-79add5f0af91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31701e1b-752e-53b5-87f8-c950c55efe89" :answer/id #uuid "31701e1b-752e-53b5-87f8-c950c55efe89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af5a6d7b-5c27-5373-b0c3-f3df6c003fb7" :answer/id #uuid "af5a6d7b-5c27-5373-b0c3-f3df6c003fb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-768c8258-584d-53be-9ed2-7212820baebf" :question/id #uuid "768c8258-584d-53be-9ed2-7212820baebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-250171" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x+3k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ find the range of possible values of $k.$\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x+3k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2}+5x+k \\\\ y &=x+3k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+k & =x+3k \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=-2k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4(1)(-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative. Setting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & <0 \\\\ 8k & <-16 \\\\ k & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-abb279d1-a081-5445-83f9-b74769bc07da" "answer-field-21fc663a-42dd-5170-baa9-33270847b513"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-abb279d1-a081-5445-83f9-b74769bc07da" :answer-field/id #uuid "abb279d1-a081-5445-83f9-b74769bc07da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-aaa25441-f037-5889-b8ee-98ed9ac7af8f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aaa25441-f037-5889-b8ee-98ed9ac7af8f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaa25441-f037-5889-b8ee-98ed9ac7af8f" :answer/id #uuid "aaa25441-f037-5889-b8ee-98ed9ac7af8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-21fc663a